Mesa lavapipe for Cosmopolitan
=============================

This directory builds Mesa 25.2.8 lavapipe, its llvmpipe CPU backend and the
required Mesa compiler/runtime code into a Cosmopolitan static archive. It uses
the separately pinned Cosmopolitan LLVM 19.1.7 MCJIT port in
third_party/llvm_cosmo. The WebGPU application can link these archives into the
same x86-64 APE executable as wgpu-native and select them with --software.

The intended deployment is one identical executable for Windows and Linux.
The software path calls the linked lvp_GetInstanceProcAddr directly and does
not load a system Vulkan loader, Mesa ICD, LLVM shared library or GPU driver.
The CPU executes the shaders. This is an experimental headless compute port;
it is not a Vulkan conformance claim or an inference engine.

Mesa's driver code and registration interface are predominantly C. LLVM is
C++, and the surrounding wgpu-native implementation is Rust. Each component
uses the same Cosmopolitan runtime and ABI in the executable.

Build
-----

From the repository root on Linux x86-64:

    bash third_party/wgpu_native/build.sh --software
    o/webgpu/ape-x86_64.elf o/webgpu/webgpu_compute.exe --software
    o/webgpu/ape-x86_64.elf o/webgpu/webgpu_compute.exe --software --matmul

For the Mesa/LLVM libraries and direct-driver diagnostic alone:

    bash third_party/lavapipe/build.sh

Python 3.12 or later, a host C/C++ compiler and ordinary source-build tools are
needed. Python packages are installed into an isolated directory under o/;
requirements-host.txt pins their versions. Host glslang 15.4.0 is built from a
verified source archive using the LLVM recipe's recorded host CMake. It generates
Mesa's internal shaders during the build and is not linked into the APE.

LAVAPIPE_JOBS controls the Mesa/host-shader-generator parallelism (default 2).
The LLVM build has its own LLVM_COSMO_JOBS setting. LLVM_COSMO_OUT and the
recipe's --llvm-metadata option can reuse an already completed, matching LLVM
build; no host LLVM dependency search is used as a substitute.

The main handoff file is o/lavapipe/LINK.json. Its link_args array contains the
registration archive, the complete Mesa archive under whole-archive flags and
the exact LLVM static closure. It also records source and patch hashes, the
direct-driver probe and the complete nested LLVM build/probe metadata. Build
paths in this generated metadata are local absolute paths. Do not copy a
LINK.json to another checkout and assume its archives still exist there.

The registration archive exports:

    int cosmo_wgpu_lavapipe_register(void);

Call this before selecting the embedded provider and creating a WebGPU instance.
The strong reference in register.c pulls lvp_GetInstanceProcAddr out of the
driver archive. The surrounding loader's embedded-provider dispatch supplies
the Vulkan loader-level compatibility behavior; no ICD JSON file is required.

Sources and local changes
------------------------

PIN.json records the complete Mesa and glslang archive URLs and SHA-256 hashes.
PATCHES.json records the checked-in portability patch and affected files.
Sources are downloaded under o/ rather than importing the entire Mesa/LLVM
trees into this repository. The complete release sources are preserved there;
unused drivers are disabled through the build configuration, not removed.

The Mesa build enables only the swrast Vulkan driver and its llvmpipe backend.
OpenGL, GLX, EGL, GLES, GBM, platform window systems, video codecs, external DRM
dependencies, shader disk cache, zlib, zstd, expat, sensors, OpenMP and external
unwind support are disabled. The necessary NIR, SPIR-V and Vulkan common runtime
code remains enabled. Mesa's ordinary internal C++ utility code is retained.

The portability patch makes the following substantive changes:

* Recognize __COSMOPOLITAN__ as a POSIX API environment while keeping Linux and
  Windows compile-time code paths disabled. Memory information and thread
  naming use Cosmopolitan's runtime OS implementations.
* Use Mesa's existing pthread timed-mutex fallback for SDK 4.0.2, which lacks
  pthread_mutex_timedlock. Per-thread CPU-time profiling follows Mesa's existing
  unsupported-platform behavior and reports zero; elapsed-clock timing remains
  available.
* Disable native dlopen/dladdr discovery, Linux FD-memory sharing and sealing,
  and RenderDoc dynamic capture. DRM header integer/format constants can still
  be included without BSD-only ioctl headers. No DRM device path is enabled.
* Omit the unused headless-surface WSI implementation. Headless compute needs
  neither a headless surface nor a swapchain, and platform surface extensions
  remain disabled by Mesa's existing lavapipe configuration guards.
* Build the final ICD target as a regular static archive. Meson includes its
  complete Mesa object dependency closure in that archive. Required weak
  entrypoint definitions are retained at final link with whole-archive flags.
* Force shader MCJIT to use the x86_64-unknown-linux-musl SysV/ELF target on every
  runtime OS. Mark all post-optimization functions with LLVM's noredzone
  attribute, including functions introduced by coroutine lowering. Windows
  does not preserve the SysV stack red zone.
* Register the linked C memory, allocation and f32/f64 math functions with
  LLVMAddSymbol during Mesa's once-only LLVM initialization. Optimizer-generated
  libcalls consequently reach Cosmopolitan's own functions. Mesa's printf,
  clock and coroutine allocation hooks already have explicit per-module maps.
* Supply a diagnostic text-stream fallback for SDK 4.0.2, which has no
  open_memstream. NIR-to-text diagnostics can use an automatically removed
  temporary file; normal shader execution and readback do not depend on this
  diagnostic path. A writable temporary directory is needed when using it.
* Move the legacy executable-memory MAP_ANONYMOUS fallback after the platform
  header so it does not rewrite Cosmopolitan's runtime constant declaration.

The paired LLVM port is responsible for executable-memory allocation,
protection and disposal through Cosmopolitan. Its source patches and standalone
JIT validation are recorded separately in third_party/llvm_cosmo and nested in
the generated LINK.json.

Validation and current scope
----------------------------

The direct-driver probe in probe.c creates a Vulkan instance through the
statically linked entrypoint, enumerates a physical device, verifies that it is
a CPU device and destroys the instance. The initial Linux x86-64 prototype
passed with "llvmpipe (LLVM 19.1.7, 256 bits)" and Vulkan API 1.4.318. This test
alone does not compile or execute a shader.

The full application tests are in third_party/wgpu_native/test.sh and its CPU
documentation. They cover integer Collatz dispatch/readback and an f32 tiled
matrix multiplication with workgroup memory and barriers. Consult the recorded
test results and CI output for which operating systems and executable hashes
have actually passed. A successful Linux build or simulated ABI test is not a
native Windows execution result.

Fresh CI run 37492821586 built the published source and passed both application
workloads on native Windows Server 2022 and Ubuntu 24.04 x86-64 using the same
executable. Collatz produced [0, 1, 7, 2], and all 143 matrix results matched
with maximum absolute error zero. Both systems reported the embedded CPU
adapter and zero native Vulkan-loader opens, and left the executable's SHA-256
unchanged. Linux also passed actual empty-root isolation and a separate
single-file bootstrap check. The tested source, executable checksum, artifact,
and job links are recorded in third_party/wgpu_native/CPU.md. Additional CPU
models and shader features require their own workload evidence.

The CPU JIT selects host instruction capabilities at runtime. Its existing
F16C and wgpu capability checks are retained. The present validation work does
not establish all optional Vulkan/WebGPU shader features, all CPU models or
half/double-precision combinations. Additional LLVM libcalls should be mapped
only with the correct ABI and semantics when a concrete workload needs them.
No native dlsym fallback is enabled for unresolved JIT symbols.

Licenses
--------

licenses/ retains Mesa's upstream license index/texts, the radix-sort license,
glslang's complete license, and Mesa-build-NOTICES.txt. The latter collects
copyright/license blocks from the actual Mesa compiler inputs and dependency
headers of this configuration. Complete original notices also remain in the
verified release source archive. The LLVM and Cosmopolitan runtimes have their
own separately retained notices. Keep the corresponding notices with binary
distributions; the source tree contains components under more than one license.
